NO LICENCE IS GRANTED BY THIS FILE.

This software is source-visible, not open source. No right to use, copy,
modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense or sell any part of it is
granted here, expressly or by implication. Access to this repository does not
convey permission to do any of those things.

WHY THIS FILE SAYS NOTHING MORE THAN THAT.

The final licence for this product has not been chosen and is not being chosen
in this file. Factory task #573 records the operator's intent — the work stays
source-visible, general use is permitted, and nobody but lucidIT, LLC may
monetise or redistribute it — and records the two findings that bear on it:
PolyForm Strict / Internal Use is the closest fit to that intent, and
BUSL-1.1 was REJECTED as too generous because it permits non-competing free
use. #573 also holds, in terms, that custom legal text must not be drafted
without counsel and that a vetted template must be adapted instead.

This notice is therefore deliberately NOT a licence. It is the default
position that applies when no licence has been granted, stated explicitly so
that nothing is granted by silence or by a placeholder.

WHAT IT REPLACED, AND WHY THAT MATTERED.

Until 2026-09-14 this file contained the MIT Licence while every shipped
manifest declared BUSL-1.1. The two contradicted each other, and the MIT text
was the dangerous half: MIT grants any recipient the right to sell, sublicense
and redistribute without restriction, which is the precise opposite of the
recorded intent, and a grant made in a public repository is irrevocable for
every copy already taken. The manifests were no better — they named a licence
this factory had already rejected.

BEFORE THIS PRODUCT IS PUBLISHED, counsel settles the licence and a vetted
template replaces this file. Publishing it with this notice in place grants
nothing, which is the correct state for an undecided question; publishing it
with the wrong licence cannot be undone.
